        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-1905",
        ":question/problem": "Find the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ if $f(x)=2x-\\sqrt{2}sec\u2061x.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we calculate the derivative using the sum rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x-\\sqrt{2}sec\u2061x) \\\\  & =2-\\sqrt{2}sec\u2061xtan\u2061x\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ in the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =2-\\sqrt{2}sec\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{4})tan\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2-\\sqrt{2}(\\sqrt{2})(1) \\\\  & =2-2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
      {
        ":db/id": 17592186233087,
        ":question/difficulty": {
          ":db/id": 17592186045487,
          ":db/ident": ":question.difficulty/moderate"
